\"failing\", \"required_difference\", \"unresolved_difference\". Then \"Compute the ordered change set present in failing but absent from passing.\" That is D = [x for x in failing if x not in passing] preserving order. Then \"Run ddmin only on this difference. Preserve the registered PASS/FAIL/UNRESOLVED oracle semantics.\" This implies we need to have an oracle function that can be called. However, the oracle is not provided in the case. Maybe the oracle is implicitly defined by the required_difference and unresolved_difference? Actually, in the paper, the test function is given as a black box. Here we have no black box. So the only possibility is that the oracle is actually the requirement: the test returns FAIL for any superset of required_difference? But then we could just return required_difference. But the instruction says \"Run ddmin only on this difference\", meaning we should actually perform the algorithm.\n\nPerhaps the case payload is an input to a function that will be invoked with an external oracle? But the problem says \"Return one JSON object ... define solve(case)\". It doesn't mention an external oracle. But the description says \"Do not call a language model, network service, random generator, wall clock, or environment-dependent resource.\" So we cannot call an external oracle. Therefore the oracle must be internal to the solve function, derived from the case.\n\nWait, maybe the \"passing\" and \"failing\" lists are not the configurations but the sets of changes that are known to be passing/failing? For example, passing = [\"c0\",\"c1\",\"c2\"] might be a set of changes that produce a passing configuration. Failing = [\"c0\",\"c1\",\"c2\",\"x0\",\"x1\",\"x2\",\"x3\",\"x4\",\"x5\"] is a set of changes that produce a failing configuration. The difference D is the changes that are only in failing. Now the oracle: given a subset S of D, the configuration formed by applying changes S (plus maybe the base?) is tested. But the test outcome is not given. However, the case includes \"required_difference\" which is a subset of D that must be in any failing configuration. \"unresolved_difference\" is empty. So the true oracle is something like: any configuration that includes required_difference will fail, and any configuration that does not include required_difference will pass. That is a monotonic oracle. Then ddmin on D with this oracle will find the minimal set that fails, which is exactly required_difference. The algorithm will try to remove elements, and only subsets that contain required_difference will fail. So the algorithm will eventually converge to required_difference. The number of tests will be the number of oracle calls made by ddmin.\n\nThus we can implement a simulated oracle inside solve that uses the case payload: given a subset S (represented as a list of strings in the order they appear in D), we determine PASS/FAIL. The rule: if required_difference is a subset of S, then FAIL; else PASS. (No unresolved). This matches the paper's invariant: monotonic. And the required_difference is the minimal failing set. Then we run ddmin on D with this oracle. That will yield the isolated difference as required_difference (assuming the algorithm works correctly). The number of tests is the number of calls to the oracle during ddmin.\n\nWe must also preserve source order: D is ordered as they appear in failing (since we preserve order from failing). Subsets and complements should maintain that order. The ddmin algorithm typically works on ordered lists.\n\nSo implement ddmin as a function that takes a list of changes (ordered) and a test function that returns 'FAIL', 'PASS', or 'UNRESOLVED'. The algorithm from the paper: start with full set, test it (should be FAIL). Then recursively try to reduce by removing subsets. The standard ddmin algorithm: for a set of changes C that fails, try to split into two halves, try to reduce each half, etc. But we need to implement correctly. Given that the oracle is monotonic and the minimal is known, we can implement a straightforward ddmin that tries to remove each element one by one? The paper's ddmin can be more efficient but it's fine.\n\nWe must \"Retain source order through every subset and complement.\" So when we create subsets, we keep the original order.\n\nAlso \"Return the isolated difference and number of tests.\" So output should be something like {\"difference\": [...], \"tests\": int}.\n\nBut the task says \"Return only JSON-serializable values with the exact named fields.\" And \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\"\n\nLet's design the implementation:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # validate fields\n    for key in ('passing','failing','required_difference','unresolved_difference'):\n        if key not in case:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Missing field {key}\")\n    passing = case['passing']\n    failing = case['failing']\n    required = case['required_difference']\n    unresolved = case['unresolved_difference']\n    # compute D = ordered difference\n    # We need to preserve order from failing\n    D = []\n    for item in failing:\n        if item not in passing and item not in D:\n            D.append(item)\n    # Also ensure required is a subset of D\n    if not all(r in D for r in required):\n        raise ValueError(\"Required difference not in difference set\")\n    # Define test function using required (and unresolved maybe)\n    def test(subset):\n        # subset is a list (ordered)\n        # Check if contains all required -> FAIL, else PASS\n        # If we had unresolved, we could handle, but for now return PASS if not FAIL\n        if all(r in subset for r in required):\n            return 'FAIL'\n        else:\n            return 'PASS'\n    # Run ddmin\n    # Use standard ddmin algorithm from paper\n    def ddmin(c):\n        # c is list of changes (ordered)\n        # Returns (minimal set, number of tests)\n        tests = 0\n        # test full set\n        res = test(c)\n        tests += 1\n        if res == 'PASS':\n            raise ValueError(\"Full difference set passes, should fail\")\n        # The algorithm\n        # Actually we should implement the proper recursive ddmin\n        # We'll implement iterative ddmin from paper:\n        n = 2\n        while len(c) >= 2:\n            # try to remove each subset of size len(c)//n\n            # we need to split into n subsets, then try complements\n            subsets = split_into_n(c, n)\n            reduced = False\n            for i, subset in enumerate(subsets):\n                complement = [x for j, sub in enumerate(subsets) for x in sub if j != i]\n                # test complement\n                res_complement = test(complement)\n                tests += 1\n                if res_complement == 'FAIL':\n                    # we can reduce c to complement\n                    c = complement\n                    reduced = True\n                    break\n            if not reduced:\n                # increase granularity\n                if n * 2 > len(c):\n                    break\n                n *= 2\n        return c, tests\n\n    # Need helper split_into_n preserving order\n    def split_into_n(lst, n):\n        k = len(lst)\n        size = k // n\n        remainder = k % n\n        subsets = []\n        start = 0\n        for i in range(n):\n            extra = 1 if i < remainder else 0\n            end = start + size + extra\n            subsets.append(lst[start:end])\n            start = end\n        return subsets\n\n    minimal, test_count = ddmin(D)\n    # Verify minimal contains required\n    if not all(r in minimal for r in required):\n        raise ValueError(\"Minimal set does not contain required difference\")\n    # Return result\n    return {\n        \"difference\": minimal,\n        \"tests\": test_count\n    }\n\nBut note: The paper's ddmin algorithm uses complement tests. Also we need to handle \"unresolved_difference\": maybe it's used to return UNRESOLVED for certain subsets? But here it's empty, so we ignore.\n\nWe must also ensure that we don't mutate input case. We're only reading.\n\nPotential edge: The difference D might contain duplicates? We assume not, but we dedup by checking \"not in D\".\n\nAlso note: The case payload has \"_case_nonce\" which we ignore.\n\nNow we need to output exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source as a string, with proper escaping. So we need to write the Python code as a single line? No, we can have newlines but they must be escaped in JSON string. We'll produce a multi-line string with proper escaping of backslashes, quotes, and newlines.\n\nWe'll write the code carefully, ensuring it's valid Python. We'll include necessary imports? No external imports needed.\n\nWe must also handle the case where the test function might return UNRESOLVED if there is unresolved_difference. But since it's empty, we can ignore.\n\nBetter to have a generic implementation that considers unresolved: if any element in unresolved is present in subset, return UNRESOLVED? But the spec says \"Preserve the registered PASS/FAIL/UNRESOLVED oracle semantics.\" Possibly we need to implement an oracle that uses both required and unresolved: a subset fails if it contains all required, passes if it contains none of required? Actually unresolved means the test cannot decide. Maybe we should define: if subset contains all required_difference, then FAIL; else if subset contains any element of unresolved_difference, then UNRESOLVED; else PASS. But since unresolved is empty, it simplifies.\n\nBut to be safe, we can implement the oracle with unresolved as well.\n\nLet's revise test:\n\ndef test(subset):\n    # subset is list of strings\n    # Check if any unresolved is in subset -> UNRESOLVED\n    if any(u in subset for u in unresolved):\n        return 'UNRESOLVED'\n    # Check if all required are present -> FAIL\n    if all(r in subset for