fference D from failing not in passing\n    D = []\n    seen = set()\n    for item in failing:\n        if item not in passing and item not in seen:\n            D.append(item)\n            seen.add(item)\n    # ensure required is subset of D\n    if not all(r in D for r in required):\n        raise ValueError(\"Required difference not in difference set\")\n    # ensure unresolved is subset of D? Not necessary.\n    # define test oracle\n    def test(subset):\n        # subset is a list preserving order\n        # check unresolved first\n        for u in unresolved:\n            if u in subset:\n                return 'UNRESOLVED'\n        # check required\n        for r in required:\n            if r not in subset:\n                return 'PASS'\n        return 'FAIL'\n    # ddmin algorithm\n    def split_into_n(lst, n):\n        k = len(lst)\n        size = k // n\n        remainder = k % n\n        subsets = []\n        start = 0\n        for i in range(n):\n            extra = 1 if i < remainder else 0\n            end = start + size + extra\n            subsets.append(lst[start:end])\n            start = end\n        return subsets\n    def ddmin(c):\n        tests = 0\n        # test full set\n        res = test(c)\n        tests += 1\n        if res != 'FAIL':\n            raise ValueError(\"Full difference set does not fail\")\n        n = 2\n        while len(c) >= 2:\n            subsets = split_into_n(c, n)\n            reduced = False\n            for i, subset in enumerate(subsets):\n                complement = []\n                for j, sub in enumerate(subsets):\n                    if j != i:\n                        complement.extend(sub)\n                res_comp = test(complement)\n                tests += 1\n                if res_comp == 'FAIL':\n                    c = complement\n                    reduced = True\n                    break\n            if not reduced:\n                if n * 2 > len(c):\n                    break\n                n *= 2\n        return c, tests\n    minimal, test_count = ddmin(D)\n    # verify minimal contains required\n    if not all(r in minimal for r in required):\n        raise ValueError(\"Minimal set does not contain required difference\")\n    return {\"difference\": minimal, \"tests\": test_count}\n\nNow check: The algorithm may not correctly handle the case where the minimal set is exactly the required subset, and the algorithm might reduce to a larger set if the complement tests fail? Actually, the algorithm tries to remove subsets. Given the oracle, the only failing sets are those that contain all required. So the complement of a subset will fail only if it contains all required. The algorithm will try to remove elements one by one. With n=2, it splits into two halves, tests complement of each half. If one half does not contain all required, its complement will contain all required (since the whole set contains all required). So the complement will fail, and we can reduce to that complement (which removes the half that didn't contain required). So it will eventually remove all elements not in required. Then the remaining set is exactly the required set (since required is contained). But note: the algorithm might keep some extra elements if they are in the same half as a required element? Actually, it should converge to the minimal set. Let's simulate: D = [x0,x1,x2,x3,x4,x5], required = [x0,x4]. Initially n=2, subsets: [x0,x1,x2] and [x3,x4,x5]. Complement of first subset is [x3,x4,x5]; test that: contains x0? No, so test returns PASS (since not all required present). Complement of second subset is [x0,x1,x2]; test that: contains x0 but not x4 -> PASS. So no reduction. Then n=4, subsets: [x0],[x1],[x2],[x3,x4,x5]? Actually with 4 subsets, size 1 or 2? k=6, n=4 => sizes: 2,2,1,1 (remainder 2). So subsets: [x0,x1], [x2,x3], [x4], [x5]. Complements: for i=0, complement = [x2,x3,x4,x5] - contains x0? no; x4? yes but missing x0 => PASS. i=1: complement [x0,x1,x4,