single.\n\nAlso, the string comparison 'FAIL' uses single quotes. Good.\n\nNow we need to ensure that backslashes are escaped: there are no backslashes in the code except maybe in newlines? When we embed the code in JSON string, we will replace actual newline characters with \\n. So we write the code as a single string with \\n for line breaks.\n\nThus final JSON:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    # validate keys\\n    for key in ('passing','failing','required_difference','unresolved_difference'):\\n        if key not in case:\\n            raise ValueError(f'Missing field {key}')\\n    passing = case['passing']\\n    failing = case['failing']\\n    required = case['required_difference']\\n    unresolved = case['unresolved_difference']\\n    # compute ordered difference D from failing not in passing\\n    D = []\\n    seen = set()\\n    for item in failing:\\n        if item not in passing and item not in seen:\\n            D.append(item)\\n            seen.add(item)\\n    # ensure required is subset of D\\n    if not all(r in D for r in required):\\n        raise ValueError('Required difference not in difference set')\\n    # define test oracle\\n    def test(subset):\\n        # check unresolved first\\n        for u in unresolved:\\n            if u in subset:\\n                return 'UNRESOLVED'\\n        # check required\\n        for r in required:\\n            if r not in subset:\\n                return 'PASS'\\n        return 'FAIL'\\n    # ddmin algorithm\\n    def split_into_n(lst, n):\\n        k = len(lst)\\n        size = k // n\\n        remainder = k % n\\n        subsets = []\\n        start = 0\\n        for i in range(n):\\n            extra = 1 if i < remainder else 0\\n            end = start + size + extra\\n            subsets.append(lst[start:end])\\n            start = end\\n        return subsets\\n    def ddmin(c):\\n        tests = 0\\n        res = test(c)\\n        tests += 1\\n        if res != 'FAIL':\\n            raise ValueError('Full difference set does not fail')\\n        n = 2\\n        while len(c) >= 2:\\n            subsets = split_into_n(c, n)\\n            reduced = False\\n            for i, subset in enumerate(subsets):\\n                complement = []\\n                for j, sub in enumerate(subsets):\\n                    if j != i:\\n                        complement.extend(sub)\\n                res_comp = test(complement)\\n                tests += 1\\n                if res_comp == 'FAIL':\\n                    c = complement\\n                    reduced = True\\n                    break\\n            if not reduced:\\n                if n * 2 > len(c):\\n                    break\\n                n *= 2\\n        return c, tests\\n    minimal, test_count = ddmin(D)\\n    if not all(r in minimal for r in required):\\n        raise ValueError('Minimal set does not contain required difference')\\n    return {'difference': minimal, 'tests': test_count}\"}\n\nCheck: The code uses single quotes everywhere, so no need to escape double quotes inside. However, the JSON string itself is enclosed in double quotes, so we need to escape any double quotes that appear. Since we used single quotes, there are no double quotes in the code string except possibly if we had used them. So it's fine. Also need to escape backslashes: there are no backslashes in the code. But there is a backslash in the line `size = k // n`? No, that's forward slash. So no backslashes. Also need to escape newlines as \\n. In the JSON string, we have actual newlines? No, we write the literal \\n as two characters backslash and n. So we need to replace each newline with \\n. In the above, I wrote the code as a multi-line string but when I put it in JSON, I must convert to a single line with escape sequences. So I'll copy the code and replace newlines with \\n and ensure no other issues.\n\nLet me produce the final JSON string with proper escaping. I'll write it as a single line.\n\nI'll manuall